1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,900
Two Pacific storms are lining up for California, and the first one arrives today with heavy Sierra snow, powerful wind gusts, and towering surf.

2
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:20,000
Hey everyone, this is your California weather update for Friday, November 13, 2026, based on National Weather Service information as of 8 AM Eastern.

3
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:30,100
Here's a number for you, the Weather Service says some breaking waves near San Francisco could reach 40 to 50 feet at favored spots.

4
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:40,200
Let's start with the big picture, because the Weather Service office in Sacramento describes this as a pair of storm systems moving through California.

5
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:50,700
The first storm runs today into early Saturday, and forecasters say it will be the stronger one in terms of both wind and heavy snow.

6
00:00:50,900 --> 00:01:00,800
After a lull during the day Saturday, the second storm arrives Sunday and continues into Monday, bringing more rain, mountain snow, and gusty winds.

7
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:11,300
The Weather Prediction Center says a deep upper level trough is pushing a front eastward, pulling a strong surge of Pacific moisture into the West.

8
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:21,000
When that moist air gets pushed up the mountain slopes, it cools and squeezes out precipitation, and meteorologists call that process orographic lift.

9
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:31,100
Now let's head up into the Sierra Nevada, where five Winter Storm Warnings are posted across California and the Lake Tahoe area of Nevada.

10
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:41,200
For the northern Sierra above 6,000 feet, the Sacramento office has a Winter Storm Warning from 10 AM Pacific today until 10 AM Saturday.

11
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:50,900
That warning includes Interstate 80, Highway 50, State Route 4, and State Route 108, plus Lassen Volcanic National Park in the southern Cascades.

12
00:01:51,100 --> 00:02:01,000
Snow totals there are expected to run 6 to 12 inches, with up to 18 inches possible on the highest peaks of the range.

13
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:10,700
Around 2 inches is possible as low as 5,000 feet, and wind gusts there could reach as high as 60 miles per hour.

14
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:19,200
Over at Lake Tahoe, the Reno office has a Winter Storm Warning from 10 AM today until 10 AM Saturday.

15
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:28,500
They're calling for heavy, wet snow, with accumulations near 12 inches above 7,000 feet and up to 6 inches below that elevation.

16
00:02:28,700 --> 00:02:37,800
Along the crest west of Lake Tahoe, totals could reach up to 20 inches, and that's where the deepest snow is expected.

17
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:47,100
And then there's the wind, with gusts of 70 to 90 miles per hour over the ridges, according to the Reno office.

18
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:56,800
For folks in Truckee and the Lake Tahoe communities, the Weather Service expects wind gusts of 45 to 55 miles per hour today.

19
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:07,300
Farther south, on the eastern side of the Sierra, Mono County is under a Winter Storm Warning from 10 AM today until 10 AM Saturday.

20
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:17,000
Mono County could see 1 to 2 feet of snow above 7,000 feet, with up to 6 inches between 6,000 and 7,000 feet.

21
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:27,500
Wind is a big part of this one too, with gusts of 60 to 80 miles per hour possible over the ridges in Mono County.

22
00:03:27,700 --> 00:03:37,600
Along Highway 395, the Weather Service expects gusts of 55 to 70 miles per hour, so if you're driving that corridor, please be ready.

23
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:46,900
Next up, the Las Vegas office has a Winter Storm Warning for the eastern Sierra slopes of Inyo County above 7,000 feet.

24
00:03:47,100 --> 00:03:57,400
That runs from 1 PM today until 4 PM Pacific Saturday, with 5 to 10 inches, and up to a foot near the Sierra crest.

25
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:07,900
The Weather Service warns that strong winds there could cause extensive damage to trees and power lines, and travel could be very difficult to impossible.

26
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:17,600
In the southern Sierra, the Hanford office has the longest warning, running from 10 AM today all the way until 4 AM Tuesday.

27
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:26,900
That warning covers elevations above 6,000 feet, including Yosemite National Park outside of the valley, plus Kings Canyon and Sequoia National Parks.

28
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:37,000
Total snow there could reach up to 20 inches, with wind gusts as high as 50 miles per hour, according to the Weather Service.

29
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:46,300
The Hanford office says the hazardous conditions will impact both the Friday evening commute and the Monday morning commute in the mountains.

30
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:56,800
Here's the bottom line for travel, if you're planning to cross Interstate 80 or Highway 50 tonight, expect winter driving conditions in the higher elevations.

31
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:06,500
Check Caltrans QuickMap for chain controls and road conditions before you leave, and carry chains, water, snacks, and warm layers in your vehicle.

32
00:05:06,700 --> 00:05:17,000
Now, a quick word on snow levels, which is the elevation where rain changes over to snow, and in this storm that matters a lot.

33
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:25,900
Most of the heavy snow is focused above 6,000 to 7,000 feet, so communities lower down may see much smaller totals.

34
00:05:26,100 --> 00:05:36,000
Moving over to the wind story, the strongest gusts outside the Sierra are focused in far Northern California this morning and early this afternoon.

35
00:05:36,200 --> 00:05:46,100
The Medford office has a High Wind Warning for central Siskiyou County until 1 PM Pacific, including Weed, Montague, and portions of Interstate 5.

36
00:05:46,300 --> 00:05:56,200
Expect south winds of 25 to 40 miles per hour, with gusts up to 70 miles per hour across most of the Shasta Valley.

37
00:05:56,400 --> 00:06:05,900
The Weather Service says damaging winds will blow down trees and power lines, and widespread power outages are expected in that warning area.

38
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:16,400
On the coast, the Eureka office has a High Wind Warning for coastal Del Norte until 1 PM, with gusts to 60 miles per hour.

39
00:06:16,600 --> 00:06:26,500
Farther south, Wind Advisories cover Humboldt, Mendocino, and Lake Counties, with gusts of 50 to 55 miles per hour along exposed ridges and headlands.

40
00:06:26,700 --> 00:06:36,200
Now in the Sacramento Valley and the Delta, a Wind Advisory is in effect from 4 AM until 7 PM Pacific this evening.

41
00:06:36,400 --> 00:06:45,900
Gusts of 35 to 45 miles per hour are expected, including the Sacramento metro area and Redding, with the strongest winds this afternoon.

42
00:06:46,100 --> 00:06:54,800
In Sacramento itself, the forecast high today is 61 degrees, with showers and thunderstorms and a 100 percent chance of precipitation.

43
00:06:55,000 --> 00:07:05,300
Tonight drops to a low of 46 degrees, and then Saturday looks partly sunny with a high near 63, so you get a short break.

44
00:07:05,500 --> 00:07:15,400
There's also a Flood Advisory until 4 PM for abnormally high tides along the Sacramento River, the San Joaquin River, and other Delta waterways.

45
00:07:15,600 --> 00:07:24,300
That covers low lying areas in Solano, Yolo, Sacramento, and San Joaquin Counties, where parks, lots, roads, and marshes could flood.

46
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:34,800
Moving over to the Bay Area, the San Francisco forecast calls for showers and thunderstorms today, a 99 percent chance, with a high of 63.

47
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:44,900
Southwest winds of 15 to 22 miles per hour are forecast, and rain and wind come through this morning and afternoon, according to forecasters.

48
00:07:45,100 --> 00:07:55,400
A Coastal Flood Advisory covers the San Francisco shoreline, where up to a foot and a half of inundation is possible in low lying spots.

49
00:07:55,600 --> 00:08:03,900
That advisory runs until 4 PM today, and then another one kicks in from midnight tonight through 6 PM Saturday.

50
00:08:04,100 --> 00:08:13,200
Now let's talk about the surf, because the Weather Service is using the words life threatening for parts of the California coast.

51
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:23,300
A High Surf Warning runs from 6 PM tonight until 4 AM Sunday along the coast from the North Bay down through Big Sur.

52
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:33,400
Breaking waves of 25 to 35 feet are expected, with occasional sets of 40 to 50 feet at favored breakpoints, says the Weather Service.

53
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:43,900
Up north, the Eureka office expects breaking waves of 30 to 40 feet along the Del Norte coast, southwestern Humboldt County, and coastal Mendocino County.

54
00:08:44,100 --> 00:08:53,200
Their warning says breaking waves can sweep people off jetties and docks, and significant beach erosion can be expected through early Sunday.

55
00:08:53,400 --> 00:09:02,500
Around Humboldt Bay, a Coastal Flood Advisory from 11 AM to 3 PM calls for 1.5 to 2.2 feet of saltwater inundation.

56
00:09:02,700 --> 00:09:11,800
On the Central Coast, a High Surf Warning starts at 9 PM tonight for San Luis Obispo and Santa Barbara County beaches.

57
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:21,500
Breakers of 18 to 25 feet are expected there, and a Coastal Flood Warning runs from 3 AM to 6 PM on Saturday.

58
00:09:21,700 --> 00:09:31,600
Forecasters say significant coastal flooding is expected with that warning, so stay off the rocks and jetties and give the ocean plenty of room.

59
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:40,500
Now let's swing down to Southern California, where storms were already firing off the coast near San Diego early this morning.

60
00:09:40,700 --> 00:09:51,000
The Storm Prediction Center noted thunderstorms with brief supercell characteristics along a sea breeze boundary from west of San Clemente to west of San Diego.

61
00:09:51,200 --> 00:10:00,700
A supercell is a thunderstorm with a rotating updraft, but forecasters stressed that severe weather was not expected and a watch was unlikely.

62
00:10:00,900 --> 00:10:10,400
They said the strongest storms could bring small hail below severe levels and locally gusty winds as they moved onshore, then weaken inland.

63
00:10:10,600 --> 00:10:20,500
At 4:35 AM Pacific, San Diego International Airport was reporting heavy rain, 61 degrees, and a west wind around 17 miles per hour.

64
00:10:20,700 --> 00:10:31,000
For today, San Diego is looking at showers and thunderstorms with a 78 percent chance and a high near 70, and showers are likely tonight.

65
00:10:31,200 --> 00:10:41,500
In Los Angeles, it's showers and thunderstorms today, an 85 percent chance with a high of 72, then rain tonight and a low of 57.

66
00:10:41,700 --> 00:10:51,200
The Los Angeles office says rain is expected in most areas tonight and off and on through early Saturday morning, then turning dry.

67
00:10:51,400 --> 00:11:01,300
Good news, the rest of the weekend looks dry in Los Angeles, with sunny to mostly sunny skies and highs in the low 70s.

68
00:11:01,500 --> 00:11:10,600
Out in the deserts, Wind Advisories cover the Mojave Desert and Indian Wells Valley, with gusts up to 55 miles per hour.

69
00:11:10,800 --> 00:11:21,100
Now across the state line into Nevada, Wind Advisories cover the Reno, Carson City, and Minden area from 10 AM to 10 PM Pacific today.

70
00:11:21,300 --> 00:11:31,200
Gusts up to 55 miles per hour are expected there, and up to 65 along wind prone stretches of Highway 395 and Interstate 580.

71
00:11:31,400 --> 00:11:41,300
Reno is forecast to see light rain today with a high of 55, then rain and snow tonight with a low near 28 degrees.

72
00:11:41,500 --> 00:11:51,000
Alright, let's look ahead, because once this first storm moves out early Saturday, California gets only a short break before the next one.

73
00:11:51,200 --> 00:12:00,300
Saturday is the lull day, with Sacramento partly sunny near 63 and San Francisco near 64 after a chance of early showers.

74
00:12:00,500 --> 00:12:10,400
Then on Sunday, the second storm arrives, with light rain in both Sacramento and San Francisco and rain chances of 86 to 91 percent.

75
00:12:10,600 --> 00:12:18,900
The Sacramento office says that second storm continues into Monday, bringing more valley rain, mountain snow, and gusty southerly winds.

76
00:12:19,100 --> 00:12:29,400
Down south, Monday brings rain back to Los Angeles with a 65 percent chance, and San Diego has an 87 percent chance of rain showers.

77
00:12:29,600 --> 00:12:38,700
And on the coast, the Eureka office says a second large swell is forecast late Sunday through Monday, after only brief improvement.

78
00:12:38,900 --> 00:12:49,200
Looking even further ahead, the Sacramento office mentions another storm with potential for more rain, mountain snow, and gusty winds mid to late next week.

79
00:12:49,400 --> 00:12:58,900
Right now, Sacramento and San Francisco show light rain Tuesday night into Wednesday, and Reno shows light snow likely in that same window.

80
00:12:59,100 --> 00:13:09,000
The Los Angeles office says more rain is likely next Thursday, and San Diego has chances of rain and gusty winds Monday through Wednesday.

81
00:13:09,200 --> 00:13:18,700
Zooming out, the Climate Prediction Center favors below normal temperatures across the West from November 18 through 22 as low pressure moves through.

82
00:13:18,900 --> 00:13:27,600
For November 20 through 26, the outlook favors warming temperatures across California and Oregon, with a slight tilt toward above normal.

83
00:13:27,800 --> 00:13:37,700
Now, what's still uncertain, snow totals in the passes depend on where the snow level sets up, and small shifts make a big difference.

84
00:13:37,900 --> 00:13:48,200
Timing and totals for the Sunday storm and the midweek storm are still being refined, and Weather Service offices update forecasts several times a day.

85
00:13:48,400 --> 00:13:58,700
As of 8 AM Eastern, there were no verified damage or outage reports from this storm, so check your utility and Caltrans for the latest.

86
00:13:58,900 --> 00:14:09,200
If you're under a High Wind Warning, secure loose items outside, charge your phone, and stay clear of downed power lines if you see them.

87
00:14:09,400 --> 00:14:18,900
And please, follow your local National Weather Service office and your local officials, because warnings can be updated or extended at any time.

88
00:14:19,100 --> 00:14:29,000
This is a developing situation with two storms back to back, so I'll have daily updates as the second storm gets closer this weekend.

89
00:14:29,200 --> 00:14:38,700
Thanks for watching, so like and subscribe and turn notifications on, and I'll see you in the next one, stay safe out there!
